    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "According to the National Cancer Institute, approximately 40% of people in the US will be diagnosed with cancer at some point in their lives. As cancer has become one of the most prevalent and harmful diseases that humans face today, it has also become an area of intense research. Cancer forms through aggressive, unchecked cell division, which is often a product of the cell cycle going unregulated or being improperly regulated. The dREAM complex, or Drosophila RBF, E2F2, and Myb complex, provides essential regulation of the cell cycle during Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly) development. The DREAM (dimerization partner (DP), RB- like, E2F and MuvB) complex in humans functions analogously, and therefore mutations in DREAM proteins are linked to many different forms of cancer in humans, making the Drosophila dREAM model of great significance in cancer research. One of the most important regulatory targets of the Drosophila dREAM complex in regards to proper cell cycle control is polo, a gene that encodes a protein kinase necessary for spindle formation and therefore proper cell cycle progression. Although it is established that polo is a target of dREAM complex regulation, the specific role that different dREAM proteins play in this regulation is not well understood. This research aims to determine how Myb, a dREAM complex activator, is able to regulate polo expression in the wing disc of third instar larvae Drosophila melanogaster. Specifically, we are asking if, in a Myb-null background, turning Myb back on later in development in the wing discs of third instar larvae will turn polo expression back on. To answer this research question, we have used a GAL4 driver system in a Myb-null background to create tissue-specific expression of Myb-RFP in varying types of wing disc cells. We then used confocal imaging to determine the resulting Polo-GFP levels in third instar wing discs. Our results demonstrate that Myb’s regulatory relationship with polo can vary in different types of cells and at different times during normal development within the wing disc, which raises future questions about how differences between cell types might play a role in this complex regulatory relationship.",
